you about the CBS and the New York Times poll. It's How has your opinion changed recently among registered voters with Barack Obama and John McCain? And you look at all these sort of figures, why has your view of McCain gotten worse as a tax on Obama 23% and again, how has your opinion changed? It's gotten better for Obama, 17% McCain, only 7% stayed the same, 76 Obama, 72 McCain gotten worse. 7% for Barack Obama, 21% voters say that their opinion of John McCain has gotten worse. How do you explain that? Well, you know, again, David, I think that there's so many polls out there on so many different subjects, and again, this isn't something that we're necessarily paying attention to. What we are paying attention to is finding the times to speak directly to the voters, whether it's at our rallies and events or it's at the debate tonight. And I think that right now you know this, and so do I, that people are concerned about the economy. Tonight's debate will focus on that, focus on domestic issues and situations, And so I think that, you know, after tonight and over the next 20 days, we feel that although we have an uphill battle, we have the right man to lead us for the next four years. And I think that when we continue to drive home this message, talk to our supporters, you know, we're confident that on November 4th, people are going to select John McCain. All right. Ben Porat, the spokesman for the McCain campaign. Ben, thanks for coming on. Thanks, David."
